# ── Build artifacts (all regenerated by scripts/setup, never commit) ──────
build/
dist/
deploy/build/
deploy/build-webapp/
layers/xrpl/build/
*.zip

# ── Secrets & generated deployment config — NEVER commit ──────────────────
# These files contain private keys (XRPL wallet seeds), live AWS resource
# IDs/ARNs from a specific deployment, or other values that are regenerated
# by the setup scripts (provision_wallets.py, setup_agentcore_payments.py,
# setup_trust_lines.py, the `agentcore` CLI). Use **/ so nested copies under
# deploy/ build output and cache directories are always excluded too.
**/wallets.json
**/config/runtime.json
**/config/payments.json
**/aws-targets.json
config/cdp_*.json
*.secret
.cli/
.cache/

# ── IDE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# ── OS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
~$*

# ── Test artifacts ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# ── Security scan artifacts (local only, contain absolute local paths) ────
.protoshield/
holmes-report/

# ── Diagram renderer ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The specs and the renderer ARE committed: README points readers at
# `python3 diagrams/render_*.py`, and a documented command whose file is not in
# the repo is worse than no command.
#
# The icon PNGs are not. They are vendor marks (the AWS Architecture Icons
# package and the AgentCore icon deck), so they are fetched locally rather than
# redistributed here — diagrams/icons/SOURCE.md is tracked and says exactly
# which file each one came from. Without them the renderers fail their own
# check_art with the missing filename, which is the right failure: loud, before
# anything is drawn.
diagrams/icons/*
!diagrams/icons/SOURCE.md
diagrams/__pycache__/

# Claude Code: the skills under .claude/skills/ are tracked deliberately (they
# are shared project knowledge), but settings.local.json is per-developer — it
# records which tool permissions THIS machine has approved. My own global
# gitignore already covers it, which is exactly why it belongs here too: the
# next person's does not.
.claude/settings.local.json
